<svelte:head><title>Hell’s Bells — Illustrated rulebook</title><meta name="description" content="Learn Hell’s Bells with illustrated card examples and a step-by-step first round. A complete, printable rulebook for 3–5 players."/></svelte:head>
<div class="rulebook-tools screen-only"><a href={base + "/downloads/hells-bells-rulebook.pdf"} download>Download the printable rulebook ↓</a><a href={base + "/gallery/"}>Component gallery ↗</a></div>
<main id="main" class="rulebook-document illustrated-book">
<section class="learn-sheet opening" id="welcome"><p class="document-kicker">3–5 PLAYERS · ILLUSTRATED RULEBOOK</p><h1>Hell’s Bells</h1><p class="book-subtitle">A game of exact promises and infernal interference.</p><div class="opening-scene"><div><h2>Welcome to the belfry.</h2><p>You are a lesser demon with a grand ambition: become Hell’s Master Bell-Ringer. Your rivals have sharp claws, strange powers, and a talent for ruining a perfectly good plan.</p><p>Your job is simple: <strong>win exactly as many tricks as you promised.</strong> No more. No fewer.</p><p>Each round, shape your hand, make your bid, and try to keep your word while the other imps meddle.</p></div><img src={base + "/art/cards/amdusias.webp"} alt="Amdusias plays his violin beneath a great brass bell."/></div><div class="promise-strip"><div><span>Ⅰ</span><h3>Shape your hand</h3><p>Discard what you don’t want.</p></div><div><span>Ⅱ</span><h3>Make a promise</h3><p>Bid your exact number of tricks.</p></div><div><span>Ⅲ</span><h3>Keep your word</h3><p>Steer your hand toward that number.</p></div></div><aside class="document-note"><strong>The best score is zero.</strong> Matching your bid earns no penalty. Too many or too few tricks costs points. At the final bell, the lowest total wins.</aside><p class="gentle-example">Bid two? You want two wins. That tempting third trick may be somebody else’s problem.</p><nav class="document-contents" aria-label="Rulebook contents"><a href="#setup">Set the table</a><a href="#tricks">Learn a trick</a><a href="#rounds">Play a round</a><a href="#powers">Meet your imp</a><a href="#finish-game">Finish the game</a><a href="#reference">Rules reference</a></nav></section>
<section class="learn-sheet" id="setup"><p class="chapter-label">01 · BEFORE THE FIRST BELL</p><h2>Set the table.</h2><p>Gather a tarot deck, the ten imp cards, and something to record scores. Separate the cards into three groups.</p><div class="component-figures"><figure><div><GameCard card={minor[9]}/></div><figcaption><b>56 playing cards</b><br/>The Minor Arcana form your hands. You play these into tricks.</figcaption></figure><figure><div><GameCard card={arcana[0]}/></div><figcaption><b>22 round cards</b><br/>The Major Arcana set the special rule and trump suit for each round.</figcaption></figure><figure><div><GameCard card={imps[3]}/></div><figcaption><b>10 imp characters</b><br/>Your imp gives you a special power. Keep the same one all game.</figcaption></figure></div><h3>A place for every imp</h3><ol><li><strong>Choose characters.</strong> Deal two random imps to each player. Each chooses one and puts it face up; set the others aside.</li><li><strong>Prepare the round cards.</strong> Shuffle 0–XIV as the regular deck and XV–XXI as the catch-up deck.</li><li><strong>Pick a random dealer.</strong> Play and ordinary bidding go clockwise. Start everyone at zero points.</li></ol><div class="suit-primer"><h3>Four suits, fourteen ranks</h3><div>{#each ['wands','cups','swords','pentacles'] as suit}<figure><img src={`${base}/art/cards/suit-${suit}.webp`} alt=""/><figcaption>{suit}</figcaption></figure>{/each}</div><p><strong>Low → high:</strong> Ace, 2–10, Page, Knight, Queen, King.</p></div><p class="margin-tip">Keep the undealt reserve, discarded cards, and won tricks in separate places. You’ll use them differently.</p></section>
<section class="learn-sheet" id="tricks"><p class="chapter-label">02 · ONE BELL AT A TIME</p><h2>Learn a trick.</h2><p>A <strong>trick</strong> is one card played by each player. The leader plays first. Go clockwise: if you have that suit, you must follow it; otherwise play any card. The winner takes the trick and leads next.</p><p>The round card names <strong>trump</strong>: a suit that beats the others. With no trump in the trick, the highest card of the suit led wins.</p><p class="example-setting">In all three examples below, <strong>Swords are trump</strong>.</p><StaticTrick cards={[minor[4],minor[9],minor[2],minor[6]]} winner={1} title="Follow the suit led." explanation="Wands were led, and everyone follows. The 10 of Wands is highest, so Player 2 wins."/><StaticTrick cards={[minor[4],minor[9],minor[28],minor[6]]} winner={2} title="Even the lowest trump wins." explanation="Player 3 has no Wands and plays the Ace of Swords. It beats every Wand, so Player 3 wins."/><StaticTrick cards={[minor[4],minor[9],minor[27],minor[6]]} winner={1} title="The wrong suit cannot win." explanation="Player 3 has no Wands and plays the King of Cups. It is neither Wands nor trump. The 10 of Wands still wins."/></section>
<section class="learn-sheet" id="rounds"><p class="chapter-label">03 · YOUR FIRST ROUND</p><h2>Build a hand. Make a bid.</h2><p>Here’s a four-player first round. Reveal a regular round card, read its rule, and note trump. Shuffle all 56 playing cards, reserve four face down, and deal 13 to each player.</p><h3>1. Discard one card</h3><p>Look for the tricks your hand might win. High cards and trump are useful, but only if you want those wins. Discard one chosen card face down. You now have 12 cards and will play 12 tricks.</p><figure class="hand-example"><div class="hand-cards">{#each [minor[0],minor[9],minor[13],minor[28],minor[41]] as card,i}<div class:discard-choice={i===2}><GameCard {card}/></div>{/each}</div><figcaption><b>A few cards from your hand.</b> Discarding a King can help if you want fewer wins. Keeping it can help if you need another. Build toward a plan, rather than simply keeping the biggest cards.</figcaption></figure><h3>2. Resolve pre-bid powers, then bid</h3><p>Resolve the round card’s pre-bid effects first, then any imp powers. Starting left of the dealer, announce the exact number of tricks you plan to win. <strong>Zero is a valid bid.</strong> The dealer bids last.</p><aside class="document-note"><strong>The dealer’s hook.</strong> Initial bids cannot add up to the available tricks. With 12 tricks and earlier bids of 2, 3, and 4, the dealer cannot bid 3. Somebody’s promise is going to go wrong.</aside><h3>3. Play the hand</h3><p>The player left of the dealer leads first. Play tricks until your hands are empty. Keep your bid in sight: once you have your promised wins, you may need to find a way to lose!</p><p class="margin-tip">The same hand can support different plans. Your bid is a promise, not a target to exceed.</p></section>
<section class="learn-sheet" id="powers"><p class="chapter-label">04 · A LITTLE INFERNAL INTERFERENCE</p><h2>Meet your imp.</h2><p>Your imp is your way to bend the rules. Read its trigger: a power might happen before bidding, on your play, or after you win a trick. Most powers are optional and usable once each round. Mark yours when spent.</p><div class="imp-stories"><figure><div><GameCard card={imps[3]}/></div><figcaption><h3>Keep them guessing</h3><p><strong>Bael</strong> plays a card face down. Your rivals still have to choose their cards before they see what you played.</p></figcaption></figure><figure><div><GameCard card={imps[0]}/></div><figcaption><h3>Change the tune</h3><p><strong>Amdusias</strong> can change the led suit with his own card. A trick that looked lost may suddenly come your way.</p></figcaption></figure><figure><div><GameCard card={imps[9]}/></div><figcaption><h3>Expose a secret</h3><p><strong>Andromalius</strong> reveals an opponent’s random card after every trick he wins. Everyone can use that information.</p></figcaption></figure></div><aside class="document-note"><strong>Example: Amdusias changes the led suit.</strong> Wands are led. Amdusias plays a Cup with his power, making Cups the new led suit. Players still to act must follow Cups if able. Earlier cards stay in place; trump still beats the other suits.</aside><h3>Read your rivals, too</h3><p>Imp cards are public. A thief may change your hand after a win; a seer may know something you don’t. Watch who has already used their power before deciding which trick to chase.</p><p>You do not need to memorize all ten powers to begin. Read the imps at your table, and look up an interaction in the reference when it comes up.</p></section>
<section class="learn-sheet" id="finish-game"><p class="chapter-label">05 · THE BELLS GROW LOUDER</p><h2>More rounds. Sharper promises.</h2><p>You receive the same number of cards each round, but discard more: <strong>one card in round 1, two in round 2, and so on</strong>. Your hand gets smaller and your mistakes become more expensive.</p><div class="round-track">{#each [[3,6],[4,8],[5,5]] as [players,rounds]}<div><b>{rounds}</b><span>ROUNDS · {players} PLAYERS</span><p>{players===5?1:2} deals each<br/>Discard and error multiplier = round number</p></div>{/each}</div><h3>Score the distance from your promise</h3><p>Multiply how many tricks you missed your bid by the round number. Too many and too few cost the same. Exact contracts score zero.</p><div class="score-stories"><div><b>0</b><p>Round 3: bid 2, win 2.<br/><strong>Exactly right.</strong></p></div><div><b>3</b><p>Round 3: bid 2, win 3.<br/><strong>One extra trick.</strong></p></div><div><b>6</b><p>Round 3: bid 2, win 0.<br/><strong>Two tricks short.</strong></p></div></div><h3>Every round has its own twist</h3><div class="arcana-stories"><figure><div><GameCard card={arcana[3]}/></div><figcaption><b>Before the finale: regular Arcana</b><p>Reveal a new regular card each round. The Empress, for example, lets you raise your bid after your first win.</p></figcaption></figure><figure><div><GameCard card={arcana[17]}/></div><figcaption><b>Final round: catch-up Arcana</b><p>Use a catch-up card for the finale. The Star gives trailing players some room for error.</p></figcaption></figure></div><p>After scoring each round, gather all playing cards and refresh powers. Pass the deal one player clockwise, regardless of scores. Everyone deals twice with 3 or 4 players, or once with 5 players.</p><p><strong>After the final round, the lowest total wins.</strong> Tied winners share victory. Some round cards recover points, but scores never fall below zero.</p></section>
<section class="learn-sheet ready-sheet" id="ready"><p class="chapter-label">06 · READY TO PLAY</p><h2>Your table-side checklist.</h2><ol class="table-checklist"><li><strong>Reveal</strong> the round card. Read its rule and note trump.</li><li><strong>Deal</strong> all playing hands and leave the reserve face down.</li><li><strong>Discard</strong> cards equal to the round number.</li><li><strong>Resolve pre-bid effects:</strong> Arcana, then imps.</li><li><strong>Bid</strong> clockwise, dealer last. Check the hook.</li><li><strong>Play</strong> the tricks and use powers at their triggers.</li><li><strong>Score</strong> your error × round number; apply the round card.</li><li><strong>Reset</strong> powers, gather all playing cards, and pass the deal clockwise.</li></ol><h3>Dealing for your table</h3><div class="deal-notes"><div><h4>3 players</h4><p>Deal 17 each; reserve 5.<br/>Play 6 rounds: 16 / 15 / 14 / 13 / 12 / 11 tricks.</p></div><div><h4>4 players</h4><p>Deal 13 each; reserve 4.<br/>Play 8 rounds: 12 / 11 / 10 / 9 / 8 / 7 / 6 / 5 tricks.</p></div><div><h4>5 players</h4><p>Deal 10 each; reserve 6.<br/>Play 9 / 8 / 7 / 6 / 5 tricks.</p></div></div><aside class="document-note"><strong>You’re ready for the first bell.</strong> Choose your imps, reveal a round card, and deal. The reference that follows covers every card and the unusual situations you can look up as you play.</aside><p>The checklist describes the ordinary round. Always follow the revealed round card’s timing; the full procedures and exceptions are collected below.</p></section>
<section class="rules-reference" id="reference"><p class="chapter-label">REFERENCE · CONSULT AS NEEDED</p><h2>Rules, powers & clarifications.</h2><p>Use this section when a card changes the ordinary game or you need an exact ruling.</p>{#each data.sections as section}<section class="reference-entry"><h3>{section.title}</h3><div class="reference-body">{@html section.html}</div></section>{/each}</section>
